WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.520
The things that make the Punisher so unique&nbsp;
and polarizing go beyond the way he deals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.280
criminals. From the influences that led to his&nbsp;
creation to the strange evolution on the page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.560
here's the in-depth, untold&nbsp;
truth behind the Punisher.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.960
There are very few comic book characters whose&nbsp;
creation hasn't been influenced by something else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.320
The Punisher was no exception. Before&nbsp;
Frank Castle hit the comics page in 1974,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.840
there was Mack Bolan: The Executioner.&nbsp;
Created by Don Pendleton in 1969,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:37.200
The Executioner was a series of pulpy adventure&nbsp;
novels that were incredibly successful.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.920
As for what they were about, well, see if&nbsp;
any of this sounds familiar. Mack Bolan was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.960
a highly decorated Green Beret whose service&nbsp;
in the Vietnam War earned him an evocative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.560
nickname. Unfortunately, his action as a soldier&nbsp;
couldn't keep him from experiencing tragedy on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.080
home front. His family was killed in a gruesome&nbsp;
crime that turned out to be tied to the Mafia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.640
prompting Bolan to go AWOL and launch&nbsp;
a one-man war against organized crime.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.600
A few years after Pendleton created The&nbsp;
Executioner, The Punisher appeared in 1974's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:16.160
Amazing Spider-Man #129. The version who appears&nbsp;
there is a bit different from how he'd evolve over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.440
the next decade, but you can see the groundwork&nbsp;
in the original story. Despite being a hired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.120
assassin working for the supervillain the Jackal,&nbsp;
he claimed to only kill those who "deserved" it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.520
and was only targeting Spider-Man because he'd&nbsp;
been tricked into believing that Spidey was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:37.840
a killer. He also mentions that he's a former&nbsp;
Marine, but now fights, quote, "a lonely war."

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.400
Originally, the Punisher was conceived as a&nbsp;
straight-up villain. In the course of writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.280
the issue, however, Conway realized that,&nbsp;
quote, "he had a moral code I could use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.800
resolve story points." As a result, the Punisher&nbsp;
simply walked away at the end of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.640
with Spider-Man getting out of the whole&nbsp;
"attempted murder" thing pretty quickly.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.160
While he wouldn't get his own comic for&nbsp;
another ten years after his initial appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.720
it wasn't long before the Punisher started&nbsp;
headlining stories of his own. Mostly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.600
this happened in the pages of magazines like&nbsp;
Marvel Preview, which were printed without the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.360
approval of the Comics Code Authority, and lent&nbsp;
themselves to more violent, adult-oriented tales.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.680
All of that changed in January of 1986 and the&nbsp;
release of The Punisher #1. Titled "Circle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:29.520
Blood," it picked up with Frank being sent to&nbsp;
prison at Ryker's Island, which, as you may know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.920
is a very large building containing nothing&nbsp;
but people who hate the Punisher. It followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:39.120
Frank as he was broken out by the Trust, a&nbsp;
clandestine organization that wanted to fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.480
him in his war against crime and spark a gang war&nbsp;
that would take out most of Manhattan's criminals.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.360
The five-issue miniseries was a success, and&nbsp;
1987 saw the launch of a Punisher series that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.960
would run for over a hundred issues and cement&nbsp;
Frank Castle as one of Marvel's top characters.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.040
The Punisher ongoing series was&nbsp;
still definitely a Marvel title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.000
but the focus was rarely on supervillains.&nbsp;
Instead, much like it had in the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
the Punisher's solo adventures drew&nbsp;
their influences from outside comics.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.920
That's a nice way of saying that writer Mike Baron&nbsp;
spent a few years basically rewriting the action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:22.800
movie aisle of a Blockbuster as 22-page comics.&nbsp;
The most egregious example comes pretty early on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.960
In Punisher #14, Frank goes undercover as a&nbsp;
substitute teacher to root out a drug dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:35.040
operation at a high school, an adventure that&nbsp;
bears some striking similarities to Class of 1984,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.520
a film that's basically just "what if the guy&nbsp;
from Death Wish was a high school teacher?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.160
To Baron's credit, though, the story where&nbsp;
Frank goes undercover with a biker gang to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.040
topple their meth empire actually&nbsp;
predates the release of Stone Cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:55.280
a cinematic flop starring ex-football player Brian&nbsp;
Bosworth with the exact same plot, by over a year.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.040
Frank Castle kills his enemies. It's the thing&nbsp;
that sets him apart from the other characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.760
in the Marvel Universe, and to a larger&nbsp;
extent, superheroes as a whole. There is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.400
however, a problem with that, at least from a&nbsp;
storytelling standpoint: if you kill your enemies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.440
they can never be developed into compelling&nbsp;
nemeses. If you get right down to it, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.800
the real world reason that Batman doesn't kill the&nbsp;
Joker. He's just too popular to go away forever.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.480
"I think you and I are&nbsp;
destined to do this forever."

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.240
That extends into another problem, in that&nbsp;
it limits the scope of Frank's enemies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.280
characters he can actually kill. The Punisher's&nbsp;
arch-enemy for the first year of his solo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.840
title was the Kingpin, and that story never&nbsp;
resolves for the simple reason that it can't.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.200
The Kingpin is too big a character to be killed&nbsp;
off by a guy who primarily fights regular human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.240
criminals. Put those traits together and the end&nbsp;
result is that the Punisher, by his very nature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.840
doesn't have many villains who&nbsp;
survive their first appearances.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.960
There are a few exceptions to the Punisher's&nbsp;
tendency to kill off the bad guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:07.200
and the biggest is Jigsaw. After originally&nbsp;
appearing in Amazing Spider-Man #162, with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:13.040
shadowy cameo in the previous issue, Jigsaw would&nbsp;
return as a bullet in Frank's side for years.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.480
Jigsaw was built around a pretty simple&nbsp;
concept: in his early days as a vigilante,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.280
Frank chucked a guy named Billy Russo&nbsp;
headfirst through a plate glass window.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.320
Billy's formerly handsome face was shredded by&nbsp;
the shards of glass, and after it was stitched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.640
up into a form that closely resembled a&nbsp;
jigsaw puzzle, he was out for revenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.120
The thing is, none of that really happens in&nbsp;
his first appearance — possibly because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:43.840
was a little too violent for 1976. Instead, when&nbsp;
Jigsaw first appears, all that stuff is backstory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.240
with the understanding that Frank has been around&nbsp;
for a while doing terrible things to criminals.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.040
As for Jigsaw, there's something of a recurring&nbsp;
gag about how he'll get his face fixed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.760
only to have it messed up again by the end&nbsp;
of the story. The strangest example of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.720
might be the time that his face was healed by&nbsp;
a mystically powerful villain named Lucifer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.720
who claimed to be the actual Devil&nbsp;
himself. That lasted about 15&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.040
pages before Frank repeatedly&nbsp;
rammed Jigsaw's face into a cactus.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:17.600
As the closest thing Frank has to a long-term&nbsp;
arch-nemesis in the comics, it's not surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.280
that Jigsaw has made it into most adaptations of&nbsp;
the Punisher, including the Netflix show. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.240
version of the character had a very different&nbsp;
origin. Rather than just being some two-bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.840
mobster, Billy Russo was an old friend of Frank's&nbsp;
that he'd served alongside in the Marines — which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.440
also made him one of the soldiers who'd killed an&nbsp;
innocent man who was framed for being a terrorist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.080
To keep his secret, Russo tried to kill Frank,&nbsp;
but you already know how well that works out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.080
for the bad guys. At the end of season 1, Frank&nbsp;
repeatedly smashed Billy's face into a mirror.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.920
Considering how violent the Netflix&nbsp;
shows can get, it's a bit surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:58.880
that Billy's scars aren't quite as grotesque&nbsp;
as his comic book counterpart's. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.240
Netflix's Jigsaw developed a fascination with the&nbsp;
masks he wore while recovering from his injuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.360
decorating them to be creepy, disfigured&nbsp;
skulls due to his nightmares about Frank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.520
He did share the original's desire for&nbsp;
revenge, though, and his success rate.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.160
By the end of the '80s, the Punisher&nbsp;
was well on his way to becoming one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.680
of the most popular characters&nbsp;
in Marvel's catalogue of heroes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.400
He also had one advantage that Spider-Man and&nbsp;
the X-Men didn't: he didn't have the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.040
superpowers that would require a massive&nbsp;
special effects budget to bring to film.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:37.200
As a result, the 1989 Punisher movie, in&nbsp;
which Dolph Lundgren starred as Frank Castle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.240
probably seemed like a safe bet for&nbsp;
low-budget success. It was not. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.280
was never theatrically released in America&nbsp;
and wouldn't hit home video until 1991.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:53.040
Looking back from 30 years later, however, The&nbsp;
Punisher '89 has its share of flaws, but it's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.640
a pretty faithful adaptation. The plot is lifted&nbsp;
from the first year of Punisher solo comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:04.480
where Frank's war against the mob has caused&nbsp;
them to seek an uneasy alliance with the Yakuza.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:10.000
The origin was changed to make Frank a cop rather&nbsp;
than a soldier, distancing him from Vietnam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.080
but that's also a tactic that different comic&nbsp;
book versions of the character have used.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.240
It's no exaggeration to&nbsp;
say that by the early '90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.800
the Punisher was one of Marvel's most&nbsp;
popular characters. If you need proof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.360
look no further than the sheer amount of comics&nbsp;
about the character that were being published.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.880
While the Punisher was starring in three titles&nbsp;
every month, characters like Captain America, Iron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:39.760
Man, and Thor all had... one apiece. The Avengers&nbsp;
had their regular title and Avengers West Coast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:45.920
The only other characters on or above Frank's&nbsp;
level at the time were Spider-Man, with four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.760
and the all-consuming, unstoppable steamroller&nbsp;
that was the X-Men, which topped out somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.880
around nine or ten, if you count everything that&nbsp;
had a reasonable chance of Wolverine showing up.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:02.080
On top of that, there were dozens of&nbsp;
miniseries, one-shots, and specials. All told,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:08.800
there were around 26 different Punisher series&nbsp;
or one-shots published between 1990 and 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:15.280
clocking in with a total of 343 issues and&nbsp;
three book-length original graphic novels. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:21.840
comparison, that's how many issues of Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man were published from 1963 to 1991.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.400
The strangest one by far,&nbsp;
however, was Punisher Armory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:32.960
a ten-issue series written and drawn by Eliot&nbsp;
R. Brown in 1990. Punisher Armory's first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.480
issue promised to reveal "His thoughts! His&nbsp;
feelings! His weapons!", and did exactly that.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.440
There was no plot, just full-page drawings&nbsp;
of guns and other assorted weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:48.080
captioned with highly detailed reviews&nbsp;
written from Frank Castle's point of view.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:51.120
Please note that almost all&nbsp;
of these were real guns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.800
and said reviews often contained Frank's&nbsp;
endorsements for real-life gun manufacturers.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.840
The weirdest thing about Punisher Armory&nbsp;
comes when you try to figure out why it exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:06.080
in the world of the comics themselves. If&nbsp;
these are Frank's thoughts about his weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.240
then who is he writing them down for?

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.400
"WHY… would you do this?"

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:17.040
Only slightly less bizarre than Punisher Armory&nbsp;
was the franchise's fourth ongoing series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.360
and the only one that didn't&nbsp;
star Frank Castle: Punisher 2099.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:28.400
Launched in 1992, the first wave of 2099 books&nbsp;
was focused on cyberpunk versions of Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:33.840
Doctor Doom, and the Punisher, along&nbsp;
with a new book called Ravage 2099.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.800
While the rest of the line was of varying&nbsp;
quality, Punisher 2099 is worth your time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:44.480
if you ever find it in a dollar bin somewhere.&nbsp;
Jake Gallows essentially had the same origin as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:50.400
Frank — family murdered by bad guys, big jacket&nbsp;
with a skull on it, etcetera — but when he found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:56.080
the original Punisher's old War Journal, he&nbsp;
decided to take up his one-man war. If Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:02.000
was an anti-hero you could understand, Jake was&nbsp;
a full-on fascist with a secret prison under his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.920
house where he kept bad guys until he executed&nbsp;
them by disintegrating them one cell at a time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.400
He would later become the director of S.H.I.E.L.D.&nbsp;
after Doctor Doom took over the world.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:18.640
By 1995, the age of Frank holding down three&nbsp;
monthly titles and flooding the market with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:24.880
spinoffs were over, but to be fair, it wasn't&nbsp;
just him. After the massive speculator-driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:30.240
boom in the early '90s that had seen comics like&nbsp;
X-Men #1 selling in the millions, the comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:35.920
industry nearly collapsed, with a contraction&nbsp;
that saw Marvel declaring bankruptcy in 1996.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:40.080
This led the company to sell off the movie&nbsp;
rights to popular characters like Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:44.240
and the X-Men, leaving them with the ones&nbsp;
nobody was really interested in. You know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.720
Iron Man, Thor, the Avengers. Who would&nbsp;
want to see a movie with those B-listers?

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:54.560
Anyway, after his titles got the axe, Frank was&nbsp;
caught up in a crossover called Over the Edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.560 --> 00:12:00.640
which, among other things, saw him having a mental&nbsp;
breakdown and killing Nick Fury. Fury got better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.720
but Frank also believed that he'd killed&nbsp;
an innocent family in Central Park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:09.280
much like his own family had been killed. That&nbsp;
led him to finally plead guilty for what we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:14.880
only assume were several thousand deaths.&nbsp;
He was sentenced to die via electric chair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:18.160
only finding out seconds before they&nbsp;
threw the switch that the family had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:23.520
been killed by Bullseye, who framed Frank&nbsp;
so convincingly that he bought it himself.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.440
It will not surprise you to find that&nbsp;
Frank's execution was a hoax. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.640
he was broken out by a crime lord who wanted&nbsp;
the Punisher to take over his empire. "Punisher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:37.840
joins the mob" was an intriguing hook, but the&nbsp;
relaunched Punisher only lasted ten issues.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:43.120
In the late '90s, Marvel was clawing its way&nbsp;
back from bankruptcy with the time-honored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:46.880
strategy of throwing whatever they could&nbsp;
against the wall and seeing what stuck.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:50.320
In 1998, however, the tide began to turn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:56.160
That year saw the creation of the Marvel Knights&nbsp;
imprint under artist and editor Joe Quesada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:00.720
who handpicked creative teams with&nbsp;
longtime collaborator Jimmy Palmiotti.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:05.200
The basic idea was to put the spotlight&nbsp;
on "street-level" Marvel characters with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:09.920
grittier direction that would hopefully propel&nbsp;
them to stardom, and it worked — so well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:15.440
in fact, that Quesada wound up becoming Marvel's&nbsp;
Editor-in-Chief two years later and Chief Creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:20.320
Officer in 2010. Looking at the four titles&nbsp;
that launched the line, it's easy to see why.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:23.600
Christopher Priest's Black&nbsp;
Panther redefined the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:28.160
introducing lasting concepts like the&nbsp;
Dora Milaje and blending the Panther's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:33.920
cooler-than-Bond world-traveling royal Avenger&nbsp;
status with street-level action. Paul Jenkins and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:39.280
Jae Lee's Inhumans was the most well-regarded&nbsp;
run on those characters since their creation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:45.040
and has kept that status in the decades since.&nbsp;
The flagship book, Daredevil, paired Quesada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:50.560
with writer and filmmaker Kevin Smith, sparking a&nbsp;
trend of recruiting from Hollywood that continues.

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:56.400
And then there was Punisher by Christopher Golden,&nbsp;
Tom Sniegoski, and the legendary Bernie Wrightson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:00.400
which is commonly regarded as the low&nbsp;
point in the character's long history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:05.760
The concept they came up with was very&nbsp;
strange, even by Punisher standards:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:11.600
after killing himself, Frank was resurrected as a&nbsp;
ghost and recruited by angels to hunt down demons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:17.040
using magic machine guns that he pulled out of&nbsp;
his magic trenchcoat. The twist was that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:22.160
celestial recruitment officer had actually&nbsp;
been his family's guardian angel, who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:27.200
been asleep on the job the day they were killed in&nbsp;
Central Park. Unfortunately, the book was a flop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.600
and a second attempt at "Angel Punisher" that&nbsp;
guest-starred Wolverine didn't improve things.

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:37.760
Two years after the failed experiment of Angel&nbsp;
Punisher, the Marvel Knights line took another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:41.760
shot at Frank, and this time, it worked so&nbsp;
well that it would become the definitive take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:46.960
on the character. Fresh from their massively&nbsp;
successful Preacher at DC's Vertigo imprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:51.840
writer Garth Ennis and artist Steve&nbsp;
Dillon took a back-to-basics approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:56.000
of pitting Frank against an&nbsp;
over-the-top caricature of the mob.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:01.840
Dillon and Ennis brought Punisher the same blend&nbsp;
of ultraviolence, grim humor, and surprisingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:06.880
deep character work that made Preacher a hit.&nbsp;
It produced what was unquestionably the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:11.520
Punisher run of all time up to that point,&nbsp;
influencing virtually every interpretation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:16.720
of the character across all media for the next&nbsp;
two decades. Ennis would wind up sticking around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:21.680
as the character's primary writer for the next&nbsp;
eight years, with a few scattered stories since.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:26.000
In that time, he oversaw the Punisher's&nbsp;
transition from the Marvel Knights line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:32.000
to the flagship of Marvel MAX. While Marvel&nbsp;
Knights had been more of a gray area, the Punisher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:37.200
of the MAX years just did not coexist with&nbsp;
characters like Spider-Man and Captain America.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:42.960
Propelled by his renewed success in the comics,&nbsp;
the Punisher would hit the screen once again in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:49.280
2004, starring Thomas Jane as Frank Castle and&nbsp;
John Travolta as the villainous mob boss Howard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:54.320
Saint. This time, the film was influenced by&nbsp;
the Ennis/Dillon run, bringing in characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:59.440
like the Russian, a nearly indestructible&nbsp;
assassin played by wrestler Kevin Nash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:05.440
Unfortunately, in terms of quality, Punisher&nbsp;
2004 is roughly equal to Punisher '89.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:10.320
While it has a little of the flavor of the comics,&nbsp;
the movie gilded the lily with some weird choices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:15.280
that dragged it down. A lot of things that didn't&nbsp;
need explaining were given detailed origins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:20.080
like getting his skull logo from a t-shirt his&nbsp;
son bought because it scared him. Frank was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:25.040
an undercover FBI agent, making his family's&nbsp;
death a revenge killing rather than the random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:30.720
violence of the original. Perhaps strangest,&nbsp;
the setting was moved from New York to Tampa.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.880
Despite the movie's flaws, Jane is a&nbsp;
solid choice, and clearly developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:38.720
a connection to the character. He&nbsp;
voiced the Punisher in a video game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:43.040
and pulled out of a sequel because he&nbsp;
didn't believe in the script. In 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:48.240
he even made a well-received ten-minute "fan-film"&nbsp;
called Dirty Laundry where he reprised the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:52.960
Even Jon Bernthal, who would go on to play&nbsp;
the Punisher on Netflix, was a big fan.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:17:00.160
A third Punisher film, Punisher: War Zone, was&nbsp;
released in 2008 from Academy Award-nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:05.360
director and former world karate champion&nbsp;
Lexi Alexander, starring Ray Stevenson as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:09.840
Frank Castle. This time, the movie stuck&nbsp;
a little more faithfully to the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:13.360
pitting the Punisher against&nbsp;
Jigsaw, and bringing in Microchip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:17.840
as Frank's sidekick. Unfortunately, there&nbsp;
were also some pretty weird choices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:22.880
notably in giving Jigsaw a brother, a cannibal&nbsp;
named Loony Bin Jim, adding what was essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:27.680
just Jigsaw-Plus to the movie instead of&nbsp;
just focusing on Frank's most enduring enemy.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:32.240
The Punisher and Daredevil have&nbsp;
been linked in comics for years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:37.280
They're frequently depicted as ideological&nbsp;
opposites, and one of Ennis and Dillon's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:42.080
memorable issues involved Frank "explaining"&nbsp;
his methods by chaining Daredevil up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:46.880
and forcing him to choose between shooting&nbsp;
Frank or letting him kill a murderer.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:52.160
As a result, it wasn't surprising when Netflix's&nbsp;
lineup of Marvel series expanded to include the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:57.840
Punisher, who first appeared in Daredevil's second&nbsp;
season before being spun off into his own show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:02.080
Despite a fairly positive reception, the&nbsp;
show was canceled — along with all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:06.640
other Netflix Marvel shows — in 2019,&nbsp;
in advance of the launch of Disney+.

